      "writtenOn": "2023-02-17T21:38:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Why is this not \u002764\u0027?\n\nThere are only 32 host events in `host_event_text`, so we can\u0027t go beyond that ([as Raul pointed out](https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromiumos/platform/ec/+/4255275/comment/941318d2_c357f53a/)). As noted in the previous comment, this will be updated `\u003c EC_HOST_EVENT_NUM_EVENTS` so we always index into `host_event_text` with a valid value.\n\n\u003e Also, can you add a comment explaining this is intentionally not EC_HOST_EVENT_NUM_EVENT so that the tool can handle older or newer ECs?\n\nThat statement is incorrect; we are updating this to `EC_HOST_EVENT_NUM_EVENT` in the next CL.\n\nThe version of the EC doesn\u0027t have any meaningful influence here, since `ectool` always needs to be able to safely index into `host_event_text`, regardless of the host event number the EC sends. While it\u0027s possible for the EC and `ectool` to have been built with different versions of `include/ec_commands.h` (and thus, different values of `EC_HOST_EVENT_NUM_EVENT`), `ectool`\u0027s version will always be correct for its own build. The values in `ec_commands.h` aren\u0027t sent at run-time to `ectool`. That means `EC_HOST_EVENT_NUM_EVENT` will always correctly indicate how large `host_event_text` is for that particular build of `ectool`, which is also enforced with the `BUILD_ASSERT` above.\n\nRegardless of whether the EC sends a host event \u003c32 or \u003e32, `ectool` will not index past the bounds of `host_event_text` when using `EC_HOST_EVENT_NUM_EVENTS` as the limit.",
